From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lagz0ne <15101444+lagz0ne@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 05:04:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 09/28] fix(canvas): a frame keeps its preview until its
 document has painted

A frame going live swapped its preview picture for an iframe at once. The
iframe is blank until the runtime has booted, taken the HTML and painted,
so every frame the camera settled on, and every frame clicked while it
showed a picture, flashed white first. Measured from a screencast of one
frame at 21% (click to select, which pins it live), three runs: a fully
white frame on screen for 68-86 ms each time, then the document.

The picture now stays on top of the booting document, in the same element
(it is not taken down and put back), until the runtime reports the
document shown. That is a new message, doop:shown, sent once per boot
after the first HTML's stylesheets, images and web fonts have settled
(bounded at 1.5 s) and two animation frames have passed, so a paint is
behind it. A frame out of view gets no animation frames and answers when
it scrolls in, which is when the swap matters. Same measurement after:
no white frame in three runs, and the swap from picture to document
changes the frame's pixels by 0.4 of 255 on average, where it is seen at
all; the element toolbar appears as soon as before (433-1158 ms against
493-777 ms, on a loaded machine).

Frames being streamed into, and canvas elements (no picture), show their
document directly as before.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
